1
00:00:00,370 --> 00:00:04,070
Should you let an AI agent run loose on your machine?

2
00:00:04,810 --> 00:00:07,610
Well, that's the question developers everywhere are grappling with.

3
00:00:08,230 --> 00:00:12,010
The thing is, AI agents are powerful in all senses of that word.

4
00:00:12,550 --> 00:00:16,990
They can get a lot done for you, but they've also been known to get things very, very wrong.

5
00:00:17,790 --> 00:00:21,050
There have been instances of coding agents, mistakenly deleting users' files

6
00:00:21,050 --> 00:00:24,170
or exposing user credentials to a malicious actor.

7
00:00:24,850 --> 00:00:27,470
And if you have to babysit it like an over-eager intern,

8
00:00:27,970 --> 00:00:30,350
you're losing half the point of having an agent in the phone.

9
00:00:30,370 --> 00:00:35,270
first place. Docker is tackling this problem with a new product called Docker Sandbox.

10
00:00:35,890 --> 00:00:40,110
So let's take a look at what it is, how it aims to solve these problems and the trade-offs

11
00:00:40,110 --> 00:00:44,930
involved in using it. If you've used coding agents, you've probably seen this particular

12
00:00:44,930 --> 00:00:50,270
problem. The constant need for attention and permissions. Agents were supposed to offload

13
00:00:50,270 --> 00:00:56,010
work from us, but instead it's asking us for permissions to do anything and everything. So why

14
00:00:56,010 --> 00:01:00,350
is it set up to be so tedious? Well, the answer is that you are the circuit-brain.

15
00:01:00,370 --> 00:01:02,830
from the risks of your agents going rogue.

16
00:01:03,330 --> 00:01:06,770
We've seen cases where an agent decides to start from scratch,

17
00:01:07,190 --> 00:01:11,830
so it runs RM-RF on your project, or even worse, your whole route directory.

18
00:01:12,730 --> 00:01:16,490
One small stumble for the agent, one giant heap of pain for the user.

19
00:01:17,650 --> 00:01:19,930
Second, your agent needs credentials.

20
00:01:20,490 --> 00:01:24,870
That means API keys, GitHub tokens, environment variables, and so on.

21
00:01:25,530 --> 00:01:29,130
A malicious dependency or a prompt injection, and those keys are gone.

22
00:01:30,210 --> 00:01:31,050
And this isn't hypothetical.

23
00:01:31,070 --> 00:01:36,050
In February 2026, researchers found vulnerabilities in Claude Code that could

24
00:01:36,050 --> 00:01:38,630
exfilterate API keys and execute arbitrary code.

25
00:01:39,210 --> 00:01:42,630
An open-course marketplace has hundreds of malicious packages.

26
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:48,460
All these risks are why permission prompts exist, and also why running AI agents with

27
00:01:48,460 --> 00:01:50,560
auto-accept is called YOLO mode.

28
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:56,060
And all this is why Docker is building sandboxes specifically for AI agents.

29
00:01:56,680 --> 00:02:01,620
The main job of a sandbox is to be a protective layer between your agents and the host machine.

30
00:02:01,740 --> 00:02:07,160
What Docker sandbox does is by wrapping your AI agent in a virtual machine that is isolated

31
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:12,380
from your host device and putting together additional quality of life improvements for using

32
00:02:12,380 --> 00:02:13,340
those coding agents.

33
00:02:13,940 --> 00:02:19,840
Now, you might be thinking, wait, Docker already has containers, why not just run the agent in a container?

34
00:02:20,690 --> 00:02:24,210
It's a fair question, and it's actually where Docker Sandbox initially started.

35
00:02:24,770 --> 00:02:30,410
But there are a few reasons why they moved away from this in Docker Desktop 4058 onwards.

36
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:35,960
The first is the kernel. Containers share the host kernel with the OS. They

37
00:02:35,960 --> 00:02:41,160
just isolate the file system and processors on top of it. That means any kernel level exploit

38
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:46,480
inside the container hits your host OS directly. For a web server, maybe that's an acceptable

39
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:53,050
trade-off, but for an AI agent running arbitrary code on your device, less so. The second problem

40
00:02:53,050 --> 00:02:58,350
is Docker itself. Coding agents need to do things like Docker build, run testing containers,

41
00:02:58,770 --> 00:03:00,710
spin-up services with Docker Compose and so on.

42
00:03:00,730 --> 00:03:05,810
But if your agent is already inside a container, how does it run Docker commands?

43
00:03:06,430 --> 00:03:08,790
Well, as it turns out, you've got two not great options.

44
00:03:09,690 --> 00:03:13,210
The first is Docker and Docker, which runs the container in privileged mode.

45
00:03:13,890 --> 00:03:17,930
This unfortunately tears down the isolation that you're trying to create in the first place.

46
00:03:18,570 --> 00:03:21,390
The second option is to mount the Docker socket from the host.

47
00:03:21,990 --> 00:03:25,730
This gives the agent direct access to every container and volume on your machine.

48
00:03:26,250 --> 00:03:27,750
That's arguably worse.

49
00:03:28,490 --> 00:03:30,710
As a result, Docker went with virtual machines.

50
00:03:30,730 --> 00:03:36,920
or VMs. A VM gives you deeper levels of isolation than containers by owning its own

51
00:03:36,920 --> 00:03:43,000
entire operating system on virtualized hardware. Think parallels or VMware running Windows on your Mac.

52
00:03:43,700 --> 00:03:49,180
A piece of software called a hypervisor act as the middleman between the guest OS and the real hardware.

53
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,960
Your host OS and everything on it is fully insulated from anything that happens inside the guest.

54
00:03:55,500 --> 00:04:00,560
That's why Docker chose VMs for the sandbox. Of course, that isolation isn't free.

55
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:06,340
our VM requires a full operating system with hardware resources, networking, package managers, all of it.

56
00:04:07,060 --> 00:04:11,540
So even Docker's micro VM requires gigabytes of memory, which is ironic.

57
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:17,260
VMs being resource hungry is exactly why Docker containers became so popular in the first place.

58
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,340
I guess in the age of AI, everything old is new again.

59
00:04:21,580 --> 00:04:23,520
That's the core architecture of the Docker sandbox.

60
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:25,760
Now, what's it like to actually use it?

61
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:29,980
Let's take a look at the practical side through the developer experience and the tooling.

62
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:34,020
Let's start with what the VM architecture means for your coding agents.

63
00:04:34,920 --> 00:04:38,280
Inside each Docker sandbox is a completely private Docker Damon.

64
00:04:38,980 --> 00:04:45,020
Your agent can build, run, and delete Docker containers completely separate from your host Docker environment.

65
00:04:45,880 --> 00:04:48,860
That means your agent can run code validation, debugging or testing,

66
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:53,520
or even spin up its own tooling as needed without any risk to your host device.

67
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:57,840
Now, the agentic tooling. Two things really stand out here.

68
00:04:58,750 --> 00:05:00,010
First, FileSync.

69
00:05:00,010 --> 00:05:05,950
Docker uses bi-directional file sync for files in your working directory, preserving your absolute parts.

70
00:05:06,510 --> 00:05:12,850
That means everything matches your host, whether it comes to relationship these files, or outputs like stack traces or error messages.

71
00:05:13,430 --> 00:05:14,990
No mental translation needed.

72
00:05:16,090 --> 00:05:20,870
Second, credential injection. Remember we talked about API keys and credentials being a risk?

73
00:05:21,430 --> 00:05:27,450
Well, Docker sandbox uses a proxy to inject your credentials into the outbound API requests on the fly.

74
00:05:28,170 --> 00:05:30,250
The agent never sees the actual token.

75
00:05:30,270 --> 00:05:36,790
So even if the sandbox is compromised, your credentials can't be stolen because they were never in the sandbox.

76
00:05:37,630 --> 00:05:41,230
Another cool thing about the architecture is that the VM is built from a Docker file.

77
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:45,080
You can customize the agent's tooling by creating your own Docker file.

78
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:50,960
And if the agent doesn't know how to use these tools, you can extend their knowledge through frameworks like agent's skills.

79
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:56,140
For example, you could add Playwright CLI to the Docker file as a headless browser,

80
00:05:56,960 --> 00:06:00,600
and then add Microsoft's own official Playwright CLI skill,

81
00:06:00,620 --> 00:06:04,820
in the project directory to teach the agent how to actually drive the CLI.

82
00:06:05,690 --> 00:06:10,790
You can also set network policies to let you control exactly which domains the agent can reach.

83
00:06:11,230 --> 00:06:16,230
I could, for example, only include my serverless elastic project domain to the allow list

84
00:06:16,230 --> 00:06:19,370
and not let my agent touch any other part of the internet.

85
00:06:20,010 --> 00:06:22,870
Now let me show you what the user experience looks like in practice.

